Summary for understanding what GPIO is:

-> GPIO is a mode of operation for the 23 I/O pins , which are controlled by their respective port registers, and I/O pins have zero or more alternate functions which can be configured by setting/clearing values of the corresponding registers. A collection of these pins are called PORTS, and the ATmega328P has 3 ports which are labelled B, C and D, which have 8, 7 and 8 pins respectively. 


-> Even when alternate functions are being used, in some cases, you might need to configure the port registers for the desirable peripheral operation. 

-> An internal pull up resistor is used when reading digital signals, and a high impedance state effectively makes the pin "floats", when the pin is set to output, the value of the PORTxn bit determines if the pin is showing a digital high or digital low. 

-> The PINx register's functionality is independent of the functionality of the DDRx and PORTx register, it is used only to read the voltage on the Pxn port pin. 

Even if the MCU is the one driving the pin , that is, even if the MCU is the one sending voltage to Pxn bit, the PINxn bit shows what voltage is there. 

When the Pxn port pin is set to output, we generally don't touch the PINx registers since we already know what voltage is present at the Pxn pin, but if the desired output is not seen, then we use the PINxn bit to check the voltage at the pin so we can determine if the issue is from the external components or from the pin itself. 









code explaination; 
1) Defining macros to write to registers across the program, simply. 

2) In the setup() we set the PB0 pin as input by writing 0 to PB0 and write PORTB as 1 to activate the internal pull up resistor, 
the code also sets up PB4 as output pin (for LED to glow when button is pressed). 

3) The main() loop is just checking the PINB0 and checking if it is 0(which means button is pressed), and turning the LED on if the status is 0 and turning off if the status is 1(since we are using negation of status for loop condition). 

4) there is also another part of the code which you can uncomment and execute which is toggling the pin if button is pressed. 

I included this code too because just writing high to LED when button is pressed results in success but if you perform toggling, then you realize that one button press is not ust one clean "0 when pressed, 1 when released" but multiple signals, since the LED won't be neatly toggling. 














